  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "For the curve $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={4}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({4}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $1$ unit up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({4}^{0})+1 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2.5).$",
